WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.513
This is the executive briefing
for Book 10 of the Federal

2
00:00:03.513 --> 00:00:07.050
Modernization Workbook, Cloud
Boundary and Landing Zone.

3
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:11.655
It's for the people who sign for
a cloud system or answer for it:

4
00:00:11.655 --> 00:00:15.062
authorizing officials,
ISSOs, CIOs and CISOs.

5
00:00:15.062 --> 00:00:17.412
The book's argument fits in one line.

6
00:00:17.412 --> 00:00:21.413
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

7
00:00:21.413 --> 00:00:26.200
A design that confuses the two fails in
front of the assessor, not in the lab.

8
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.075
In the next thirty minutes
I'll take four questions.

9
00:00:29.075 --> 00:00:32.417
Why the authorization boundary
decides what you can use,

10
00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:34.550
and why so much of it stays invisible.

11
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:39.675
What changed at FedRAMP in 2026, and what
Government Community Cloud leaves out.

12
00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:41.113
Who owns the boundary.

13
00:00:41.113 --> 00:00:44.332
And how to respond when a
capability falls outside it,

14
00:00:44.332 --> 00:00:48.137
and evidence that response in
your own authorization package.

15
00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:50.625
There's no configuration on these slides.

16
00:00:50.625 --> 00:00:55.670
Each decision slide says what the decision
is, who owns it, and when you need it.

17
00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.470
Start with two agencies holding
the same Microsoft 365 license.

18
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.208
One runs a commercial tenant.

19
00:01:03.208 --> 00:01:06.750
Its admin center shows
per-location network assessments,

20
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:11.546
informed network routing to its SD-WAN,
and an Adoption Score for its workforce.

21
00:01:11.546 --> 00:01:14.957
The other runs a GCC tenant
and sees none of them,

22
00:01:14.957 --> 00:01:19.332
because Microsoft documents
each one as unavailable there.

23
00:01:19.332 --> 00:01:21.924
Nothing in the license,
the price list or the

24
00:01:21.924 --> 00:01:25.307
commercial documentation
tells the second agency that.

25
00:01:25.307 --> 00:01:30.049
The book's explanation is that three
separate decisions, made by three different

26
00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:34.083
parties, have to line up before a
feature is usable in a federal system.

27
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:35.957
First, the certification.

28
00:01:35.957 --> 00:01:40.760
FedRAMP certifies a specific cloud
service offering at a certification class,

29
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.307
and the FedRAMP Marketplace is the record.

30
00:01:43.307 --> 00:01:46.911
A feature outside the certified
offering isn't covered by it,

31
00:01:46.911 --> 00:01:48.496
whatever the portal shows.

32
00:01:48.496 --> 00:01:50.370
Second, the environment.

33
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:55.898
Microsoft runs GCC, GCC High and DoD with
features that differ from commercial,

34
00:01:55.898 --> 00:02:01.170
and it publishes those differences one
feature at a time, on separate pages.

35
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.520
There's no single machine-readable list.

36
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.357
Third, the agency's own authorization.

37
00:02:06.357 --> 00:02:10.775
The authorizing official accepts the
risk of the agency's specific use:

38
00:02:10.775 --> 00:02:14.445
the configuration selected
and the integrations enabled.

39
00:02:14.445 --> 00:02:17.301
FedRAMP says an agency
should never create a

40
00:02:17.301 --> 00:02:21.007
standalone authorization to
operate for a cloud service.

41
00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:24.483
It authorizes its own system,
which uses the service.

42
00:02:24.483 --> 00:02:27.770
What you can actually use is
the overlap of all three.

43
00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.470
So the first decision is simple and cheap.

44
00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.478
Before the next architecture
review or authorization milestone,

45
00:02:34.478 --> 00:02:39.433
require every cloud feature a design
depends on to be listed with its Marketplace

46
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:43.732
package, its availability in your
environment, and the page that says so.

47
00:02:43.732 --> 00:02:48.482
Workload owners declare the dependencies,
and the system owner checks each one.

48
00:02:48.482 --> 00:02:52.950
A feature with no citation is
unverified, not available.

49
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.400
Why does nobody see this coming?

50
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.050
Chapter 02 gives four reasons.

51
00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.463
Surfaces are shared.

52
00:02:59.463 --> 00:03:04.582
Commercial and government tenants use
the same admin centers and much of the

53
00:03:04.582 --> 00:03:09.862
same documentation, and Intune in GCC is
the same instance as commercial Intune.

54
00:03:09.862 --> 00:03:14.774
Exclusions are scattered, usually one
sentence on each feature's own page.

55
00:03:14.774 --> 00:03:21.488
Features move: Microsoft retired Adoption
Score's technology-experience categories

56
00:03:21.488 --> 00:03:25.591
for every tenant in early
2026, so a gap list written in

57
00:03:25.591 --> 00:03:29.600
2025 can name a GCC loss that's
now a loss for everyone.

58
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.800
And absence doesn't announce itself.

59
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.900
A detection whose input never arrives
produces the same dashboard as a quiet day.

60
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.520
An agency that builds a Zero
Trust control on a signal its

61
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.062
environment doesn't deliver
hasn't built a weak control.

62
00:03:44.062 --> 00:03:47.226
It has built no control,
and nothing tells it so.

63
00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:49.527
That's why discovery tends to come late,

64
00:03:49.527 --> 00:03:52.837
during an authorization cycle
or an analytics rollout.

65
00:03:52.837 --> 00:03:54.596
And the problem is structural:

66
00:03:54.596 --> 00:03:58.463
no tenant setting turns on a feature
the environment doesn't offer.

67
00:03:58.463 --> 00:04:00.850
But the opposite error is just as costly.

68
00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:06.093
A widely repeated example listed five
features as silently missing from GCC.

69
00:04:06.093 --> 00:04:09.533
Checked against Microsoft's
documentation, one is:

70
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:13.875
the Locations view of network
connectivity in the admin center.

71
00:04:13.875 --> 00:04:19.525
Device Health Attestation and expedited
updates are GCC High and DoD gaps.

72
00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:23.163
Endpoint analytics isn't
a documented GCC gap.

73
00:04:23.163 --> 00:04:27.488
And Azure Arc is an Azure service,
in Azure's FedRAMP High scope.

74
00:04:27.488 --> 00:04:33.300
An unverified boundary story sends an agency
to build compensations it doesn't need.

75
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.375
The program's own register of
GCC telemetry gaps has 26 rows.

76
00:04:38.375 --> 00:04:41.862
Eight are vendor-confirmed;
fifteen are inference.

77
00:04:41.862 --> 00:04:47.088
So rebuild any gap list you rely on
so every row cites a vendor page.

78
00:04:47.088 --> 00:04:51.740
The system owner keeps it, and the
CISO decides what counts as evidence.

79
00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:53.915
What changed at FedRAMP?

80
00:04:53.915 --> 00:04:58.845
FedRAMP announced its Consolidated
Rules for 2026 on June 25,

81
00:04:58.845 --> 00:05:02.615
2026, and they rename the
product of the program.

82
00:05:02.615 --> 00:05:06.055
A cloud service offering
is now FedRAMP Certified,

83
00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:08.430
at a certification class from A to D.

84
00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:13.098
FedRAMP's February 2026 notice maps
the classes to the old baselines:

85
00:05:13.098 --> 00:05:16.292
Class B covers the former
low-impact baselines,

86
00:05:16.292 --> 00:05:21.452
Class C includes the current Moderate
baseline, and Class D the current High.

87
00:05:21.452 --> 00:05:23.828
It dropped numbered levels on purpose,

88
00:05:23.828 --> 00:05:27.764
to avoid confusion with the
Defense Department's impact levels.

89
00:05:27.764 --> 00:05:30.090
Two cautions come with the new labels.

90
00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:33.469
The class describes what
FedRAMP assessed, not your use:

91
00:05:33.469 --> 00:05:37.068
agencies may authorize a
service at any security category

92
00:05:37.068 --> 00:05:40.740
they deem appropriate under
the Risk Management Framework.

93
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.540
And certification doesn't
replace your decision.

94
00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.535
Each agency still authorizes its
own federal information systems,

95
00:05:47.535 --> 00:05:51.815
and notifies FedRAMP afterward with
a copy of the authorization letter.

96
00:05:51.815 --> 00:05:53.465
The dates matter for planning.

97
00:05:53.465 --> 00:05:57.865
Optional early adoption
began on July 4, 2026.

98
00:05:57.865 --> 00:06:02.977
Adoption is mandatory for
everyone on January 1, 2027.

99
00:06:02.977 --> 00:06:08.717
FedRAMP stops accepting new Rev5
certification requests on June 11, 2027,

100
00:06:08.717 --> 00:06:13.893
and says the end date for existing
Rev5 certifications isn't final,

101
00:06:13.893 --> 00:06:16.340
but is likely no later than 2029.

102
00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:20.940
Plan against those words, not against
a specific date someone repeats.

103
00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.165
So for years you'll hold two
kinds of package at once.

104
00:06:24.165 --> 00:06:27.665
Rev5 packages describe a
boundary the provider drew.

105
00:06:27.665 --> 00:06:31.252
Packages under the new rules
describe scope, as data.

106
00:06:31.252 --> 00:06:34.316
And the new rule families
on assessment scope,

107
00:06:34.316 --> 00:06:39.639
change notification and data sharing
apply to Rev5 Class C certifications too.

108
00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:45.200
Read each package in its own terms, and
don't force one vocabulary into the other.

109
00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:48.900
The rules also changed what
the word boundary means.

110
00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.896
They're published as one
machine-readable ruleset,

111
00:06:51.896 --> 00:06:55.462
so the vocabulary can be counted
rather than characterized.

112
00:06:55.462 --> 00:07:04.499
In version 2026.10.13.01, the phrase
authorization boundary occurs zero times.

113
00:07:04.499 --> 00:07:09.595
The word boundary occurs eight times:
three times about the agency's own system,

114
00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:14.062
four times as a tag on rule families,
and once in supply-chain guidance.

115
00:07:14.062 --> 00:07:17.525
Those four families say what
a provider's boundary now is.

116
00:07:17.525 --> 00:07:20.450
Minimum Assessment Scope,
what is in the offering.

117
00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.500
Cryptographic Module Use.

118
00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.125
Vulnerability Detection and Response.

119
00:07:25.125 --> 00:07:28.712
And Certification Data Sharing,
how the evidence reaches you.

120
00:07:28.712 --> 00:07:32.199
Four sets of obligations,
not a line on a diagram.

121
00:07:32.199 --> 00:07:35.975
The only boundary the rules name
is the agency's own system.

122
00:07:35.975 --> 00:07:39.925
Minimum Assessment Scope is a
test about data, not topology.

123
00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:43.212
A resource is in the offering
if it's likely to handle

124
00:07:43.212 --> 00:07:47.188
federal customer data or affect
its security, wherever it sits.

125
00:07:47.188 --> 00:07:52.248
And resources may vary by security category,
so a refusal that rests on "it would

126
00:07:52.248 --> 00:07:57.463
raise the whole environment's level" now
has to show which resource and which data.

127
00:07:57.463 --> 00:08:00.750
One line in that guidance
matters to every estate.

128
00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:04.355
Software delivered for
installation on agency systems,

129
00:08:04.355 --> 00:08:08.727
and not operated in a shared-responsibility
model, typically agents,

130
00:08:08.727 --> 00:08:13.713
application clients and mobile apps, is
entirely outside the scope of FedRAMP.

131
00:08:13.713 --> 00:08:15.474
That doesn't make it unassessed.

132
00:08:15.474 --> 00:08:16.663
It makes it yours.

133
00:08:16.663 --> 00:08:21.433
So decide who owns each agent and
client, and cover it in the agency's

134
00:08:21.433 --> 00:08:26.550
own authorization: its configuration,
its updates and the data it sends.

135
00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:31.475
The authorizing official draws the boundary;
endpoint engineering does the work.

136
00:08:31.475 --> 00:08:34.228
Settle it at the next package update,

137
00:08:34.228 --> 00:08:38.810
and before the rules become
mandatory on January 1, 2027.

138
00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:42.104
Under the old model, a
significant change to a

139
00:08:42.104 --> 00:08:44.922
certified service went
back through an approval.

140
00:08:44.922 --> 00:08:49.359
The Significant Change Notification
rules replace that with notification,

141
00:08:49.359 --> 00:08:52.409
scaled to risk, and the
deadlines are in business days.

142
00:08:52.409 --> 00:08:55.472
The provider classifies
every potential change.

143
00:08:55.472 --> 00:08:59.044
A change of certification
class needs a new assessment;

144
00:08:59.044 --> 00:09:01.373
it can't be handled as a notification.

145
00:09:01.373 --> 00:09:05.519
Routine recurring changes, such as
patching and signature updates,

146
00:09:05.519 --> 00:09:07.148
get no formal notice at all.

147
00:09:07.148 --> 00:09:11.497
Adaptive changes are notified within
10 business days after they finish.

148
00:09:11.497 --> 00:09:15.258
Transformative changes, the
ones with substantive new risk,

149
00:09:15.258 --> 00:09:19.243
need initial plans at least 30
business days before work starts,

150
00:09:19.243 --> 00:09:21.499
final plans 10 business days before,

151
00:09:21.499 --> 00:09:26.236
and notices within 5 business days
after finishing and after verification.

152
00:09:26.236 --> 00:09:31.585
Every notice must be human-readable and
JSON, and providers keep 12 months of them.

153
00:09:31.585 --> 00:09:33.460
Evidence becomes a feed too.

154
00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.875
Providers must use a
FedRAMP-compatible trust center,

155
00:09:36.875 --> 00:09:40.973
with documented programmatic
access to all certification data.

156
00:09:40.973 --> 00:09:49.210
On October 21, 2026, only 110 of 781
Marketplace listings had a trust-center entry.

157
00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.110
There are two consequences for you.

158
00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:55.679
A provider that ships continuously can
treat most releases as routine and file

159
00:09:55.679 --> 00:09:58.379
nothing, so keeping
current with commercial no

160
00:09:58.379 --> 00:10:01.010
longer costs the provider
a compliance cycle.

161
00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.347
But your review duty doesn't shrink.

162
00:10:03.347 --> 00:10:07.029
Agencies must review each
Ongoing Certification Report for

163
00:10:07.029 --> 00:10:10.998
changes that affect the risk
accepted in their own authorization.

164
00:10:10.998 --> 00:10:12.547
So name the reviewer now.

165
00:10:12.547 --> 00:10:17.640
FedRAMP recommends designating a federal
senior information security official;

166
00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.109
in the program's model that's
the CISO or a named delegate.

167
00:10:21.109 --> 00:10:24.779
The ISSO reads each notice
against the boundary record,

168
00:10:24.779 --> 00:10:30.090
and the authorizing official decides
whether it changes the agency's risk.

169
00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.503
Now the feature gap itself.

170
00:10:32.503 --> 00:10:35.795
Chapter 04 reads GCC from
Microsoft's own documents,

171
00:10:35.795 --> 00:10:40.927
and they don't describe a compliance
perimeter that dims the commercial service.

172
00:10:40.927 --> 00:10:43.750
GCC has no endpoint list of its own:

173
00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:48.690
Microsoft publishes one list
for Worldwide, including GCC.

174
00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:53.427
The restricted, separately networked
environment begins at GCC High.

175
00:10:53.427 --> 00:10:58.152
What GCC has is a list of exclusions,
and the list isn't a ladder.

176
00:10:58.152 --> 00:11:02.584
Network connectivity in the
admin center, Adoption Score,

177
00:11:02.584 --> 00:11:06.739
the Office 365 content
delivery network and informed

178
00:11:06.739 --> 00:11:11.078
network routing are missing in
GCC, GCC High and DoD alike.

179
00:11:11.078 --> 00:11:14.328
Windows release health,
Word version history and

180
00:11:14.328 --> 00:11:17.341
Excel Office Scripts
reach GCC and stop there.

181
00:11:17.341 --> 00:11:19.515
Moving up doesn't restore everything.

182
00:11:19.515 --> 00:11:23.515
The largest documented loss is
the network connectivity layer.

183
00:11:23.515 --> 00:11:26.962
A commercial tenant gets,
at no extra license cost,

184
00:11:26.962 --> 00:11:30.998
a daily network assessment from
0 to 100 for each location,

185
00:11:30.998 --> 00:11:36.464
with 80 as the healthy baseline; a view of
each location's egress and front doors;

186
00:11:36.464 --> 00:11:41.677
insights such as detecting TLS
interception; and performance attributed

187
00:11:41.677 --> 00:11:45.965
to named network providers,
whether ISP, overlay or corporate.

188
00:11:45.965 --> 00:11:50.384
An agency choosing between SASE or
transport providers in a commercial

189
00:11:50.384 --> 00:11:54.803
tenant gets comparative evidence from
Microsoft, in its own admin center.

190
00:11:54.803 --> 00:11:58.078
The same agency in GCC gets none of it.

191
00:11:58.078 --> 00:12:03.355
Microsoft's network connectivity test
tool does support GCC, run locally from

192
00:12:03.355 --> 00:12:08.389
each location, and it closes part of the
gap, but not the provider comparison.

193
00:12:08.389 --> 00:12:10.640
So this decision is a budget line.

194
00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:14.012
Fund the independent measurement
that replaces the layer,

195
00:12:14.012 --> 00:12:16.627
before you pick a
transport or SASE provider.

196
00:12:16.627 --> 00:12:21.010
That's the sponsor and CIO's call,
because they fund the platform.

197
00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:26.526
Much federal reasoning runs two questions
together: GCC is Moderate, Moderate

198
00:12:26.526 --> 00:12:30.998
is why the feature is missing, and a
High certification would bring it back.

199
00:12:30.998 --> 00:12:33.497
The Marketplace doesn't
support that chain.

200
00:12:33.497 --> 00:12:40.809
Read on October 21, 2026, it lists
three Microsoft 365 government packages.

201
00:12:40.809 --> 00:12:45.383
The GCC package agencies run is
FedRAMP Certified at Class C,

202
00:12:45.383 --> 00:12:48.035
Moderate, with 100 authorizations.

203
00:12:48.035 --> 00:12:53.485
A second package with the identical name
is In Process at Class D, High, with none.

204
00:12:53.485 --> 00:12:58.735
And GCC High is a separate package,
certified at Class D, with 5.

205
00:12:58.735 --> 00:13:03.230
The straightforward reading of that
second package is a High effort for GCC,

206
00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.298
but the Marketplace doesn't
state how the two relate,

207
00:13:06.298 --> 00:13:08.510
or whether one will replace the other.

208
00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:11.685
Treat it as a question for
the provider, not a roadmap.

209
00:13:11.685 --> 00:13:15.403
Microsoft's own GCC service
description adds a wrinkle:

210
00:13:15.403 --> 00:13:20.091
it says Office 365 Government supports
FedRAMP at a High impact level,

211
00:13:20.091 --> 00:13:22.920
while the Marketplace record says Class C.

212
00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:27.285
Cite the Marketplace and the
package, and record the discrepancy.

213
00:13:27.285 --> 00:13:28.473
Now the key point.

214
00:13:28.473 --> 00:13:35.198
Network connectivity is missing in GCC
High too, and GCC High is already Class D.

215
00:13:35.198 --> 00:13:38.547
A higher class changes what data
you may place in the service.

216
00:13:38.547 --> 00:13:42.660
It doesn't, by itself, restore
a feature the provider excluded.

217
00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:46.155
An agency that defers an equivalent
because High is coming may

218
00:13:46.155 --> 00:13:49.785
wait for something the certification
was never going to deliver.

219
00:13:49.785 --> 00:13:53.198
So plan against the class of
the package you actually use.

220
00:13:53.198 --> 00:13:57.013
And don't hard-code Moderate into
a multi-year architecture either,

221
00:13:57.013 --> 00:13:58.747
because that premise may move.

222
00:13:58.747 --> 00:14:01.347
The sponsor and CIO own that honest plan,

223
00:14:01.347 --> 00:14:05.090
in the current architecture
and acquisition cycle.

224
00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:07.028
Who owns all this?

225
00:14:07.028 --> 00:14:12.328
Chapter 08's rule is one decider per
row, and it starts from FedRAMP's split.

226
00:14:12.328 --> 00:14:15.840
The provider owns the service
boundary and its certification.

227
00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:18.722
The agency owns the
boundary of its own system,

228
00:14:18.722 --> 00:14:22.515
and everything it builds,
configures and connects inside it.

229
00:14:22.515 --> 00:14:26.265
On the agency side, the
authorizing official determines the

230
00:14:26.265 --> 00:14:29.865
authorization boundary, with
input from the system owner.

231
00:14:29.865 --> 00:14:33.515
That comes straight from NIST SP 800-37.

232
00:14:33.515 --> 00:14:38.080
The official signs every documented
exception, approves the plan of action

233
00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:42.427
and milestones, and decides whether
a change requires reauthorization.

234
00:14:42.427 --> 00:14:45.133
Explicit risk acceptance
can't be delegated,

235
00:14:45.133 --> 00:14:49.740
and for federal agencies the role is
assigned to government personnel only.

236
00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:52.929
A designated representative
can run the process;

237
00:14:52.929 --> 00:14:55.340
only the authorizing official signs.

238
00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:57.768
The system owner is the boundary owner:

239
00:14:57.768 --> 00:15:00.652
keeper of the boundary
record and the package.

240
00:15:00.652 --> 00:15:05.314
The CISO sets the control requirements
and decides what counts as evidence,

241
00:15:05.314 --> 00:15:08.664
and the ISSO reads provider
notices against the record.

242
00:15:08.664 --> 00:15:12.254
The sponsor and CIO choose
the clouds, fund the platform,

243
00:15:12.254 --> 00:15:15.686
set the sequence and break
the ties, which will happen,

244
00:15:15.686 --> 00:15:19.353
because everyone can reasonably
claim the landing zone.

245
00:15:19.353 --> 00:15:21.040
Then the people who do the work.

246
00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:25.857
The platform team builds the landing
zone, the policy and subscription vending,

247
00:15:25.857 --> 00:15:28.777
but it doesn't decide
whether a gap is acceptable.

248
00:15:28.777 --> 00:15:31.014
That goes to the authorizing official.

249
00:15:31.014 --> 00:15:34.602
Workload owners declare the
features their designs depend on.

250
00:15:34.602 --> 00:15:37.439
Security operations runs drift detection.

251
00:15:37.439 --> 00:15:39.541
And acquisition holds the contract,

252
00:15:39.541 --> 00:15:43.602
because only a contracting officer
can change what the agency bought.

253
00:15:43.602 --> 00:15:46.277
FedRAMP is consulted, not in charge.

254
00:15:46.277 --> 00:15:48.496
Agencies notify it after authorizing,

255
00:15:48.496 --> 00:15:52.727
and work with it when a determination
conflicts with a provider's package.

256
00:15:52.727 --> 00:15:56.866
Its certification is an input to
the authorizing official's decision.

257
00:15:56.866 --> 00:15:58.700
It never replaces it.

258
00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:03.888
The most consequential boundary decisions
don't look like boundary decisions.

259
00:16:03.888 --> 00:16:05.643
They look like engineering tickets,

260
00:16:05.643 --> 00:16:08.512
and that's how they slip past
the people who should make them.

261
00:16:08.512 --> 00:16:09.363
Placement.

262
00:16:09.363 --> 00:16:13.801
Vending a new subscription is the
platform team's mechanism, but whether

263
00:16:13.801 --> 00:16:18.313
a workload is inside the authorized
system is the system owner's decision.

264
00:16:18.313 --> 00:16:21.523
The cheapest way to turn
an authorized system into

265
00:16:21.523 --> 00:16:24.963
an unauthorized one is a
subscription no one recorded.

266
00:16:24.963 --> 00:16:29.374
A workload whose subscription isn't in
the boundary record doesn't go live.

267
00:16:29.374 --> 00:16:30.663
Feature dependency.

268
00:16:30.663 --> 00:16:34.412
The system owner checks each
feature a design depends on against

269
00:16:34.412 --> 00:16:38.162
what's been tested to work in your
cloud, before the design is built.

270
00:16:38.162 --> 00:16:39.250
The contract.

271
00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:44.199
The GCC listing says customers must
buy a GCC SKU to be covered by the GCC

272
00:16:44.199 --> 00:16:48.226
authorization, so a licensing
choice is a boundary choice,

273
00:16:48.226 --> 00:16:51.162
and it belongs to the contracting officer.

274
00:16:51.162 --> 00:16:52.475
Provider changes.

275
00:16:52.475 --> 00:16:56.781
When a provider notice might change
the agency's risk, the authorizing

276
00:16:56.781 --> 00:17:01.012
official decides, not whoever happens
to be on the distribution list.

277
00:17:01.012 --> 00:17:02.287
And asking for more.

278
00:17:02.287 --> 00:17:07.657
Agencies must not require a certified
service to supply more than FedRAMP requires,

279
00:17:07.657 --> 00:17:13.026
unless the agency head or a delegate finds
a demonstrable need and notifies FedRAMP.

280
00:17:13.026 --> 00:17:17.100
The landing zone makes these decisions
checkable, but it doesn't make them.

281
00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:20.938
It enforces structure, and its
compliance reports are evidence,

282
00:17:20.938 --> 00:17:24.775
yet Microsoft itself calls the
policy compliance view partial.

283
00:17:24.775 --> 00:17:26.700
Two planning facts for leaders.

284
00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:31.113
Microsoft's landing-zone accelerators
are supported only in public Azure,

285
00:17:31.113 --> 00:17:34.600
so an Azure Government build
needs the code adapted by hand.

286
00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:38.655
And the pipeline that builds it is
inside the boundary question too,

287
00:17:38.655 --> 00:17:41.262
because Terraform state is sensitive data.

288
00:17:41.262 --> 00:17:43.790
Plan both; don't discover them.

289
00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:47.365
How do you respond when
a capability is outside?

290
00:17:47.365 --> 00:17:53.353
NIST SP 800-37 names four options: employ
compensating controls, accept greater

291
00:17:53.353 --> 00:17:58.978
risk, contract with a more trustworthy
provider, or don't obtain the service.

292
00:17:58.978 --> 00:18:04.278
The book turns them into three responses,
and each one is a decision with an owner.

293
00:18:04.278 --> 00:18:06.515
One: compensate in-boundary.

294
00:18:06.515 --> 00:18:09.160
Rebuild the capability, or enough of it,

295
00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:13.127
from signals inside the boundary,
and say that it's derived.

296
00:18:13.127 --> 00:18:15.315
Take the network connectivity gap.

297
00:18:15.315 --> 00:18:20.093
Scheduled runs of Microsoft's test tool
from each location close part of it,

298
00:18:20.093 --> 00:18:22.328
if the results are labeled derived.

299
00:18:22.328 --> 00:18:26.377
They don't replace the provider
comparison, and the register should say so.

300
00:18:26.377 --> 00:18:31.553
A narrative that hides the substitution is
exactly the kind an assessor takes apart.

301
00:18:31.553 --> 00:18:33.827
Two: a documented exception.

302
00:18:33.827 --> 00:18:37.399
Either a request through the
provider's own exception process,

303
00:18:37.399 --> 00:18:41.314
or the authorizing official accepting
the residual risk in writing.

304
00:18:41.314 --> 00:18:45.400
A gap you intend to close goes on
the plan of action and milestones,

305
00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:47.215
with a milestone and an owner.

306
00:18:47.215 --> 00:18:51.897
A gap you've decided to live with goes
into a signed exception with an end or

307
00:18:51.897 --> 00:18:56.578
review date, so that permanent is a
decision someone repeats, not a default.

308
00:18:56.578 --> 00:18:58.214
Three: an alternative path,

309
00:18:58.214 --> 00:19:02.228
a different certified service or
a different way to meet the need.

310
00:19:02.228 --> 00:19:06.452
And going without is legitimate
too, as long as someone decided it.

311
00:19:06.452 --> 00:19:08.403
Before any of these, probe.

312
00:19:08.403 --> 00:19:13.160
A scripted test of what actually works in
your own tenant, checking for the output

313
00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:17.990
rather than the switch, rerun on a schedule
and after any relevant provider notice.

314
00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:22.539
So for each gap, the system owner
proposes a response, the authorizing

315
00:19:22.539 --> 00:19:27.328
official decides every exception,
and the CISO sets the evidence class.

316
00:19:27.328 --> 00:19:32.380
Make that call per register row, before
a design depends on the capability.

317
00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:34.918
Two cautions on exceptions.

318
00:19:34.918 --> 00:19:36.967
First, the provider's path.

319
00:19:36.967 --> 00:19:41.318
ExpressRoute for Microsoft
365 is the worked example.

320
00:19:41.318 --> 00:19:43.641
Microsoft says it doesn't recommend it,

321
00:19:43.641 --> 00:19:48.142
and authorizes it only when a customer's
regulatory requirement mandates

322
00:19:48.142 --> 00:19:52.643
direct connectivity, with the text
excerpt and a web link to the regulation.

323
00:19:52.643 --> 00:19:56.992
Federal readers often assume
FedRAMP or FISMA supplies that hook.

324
00:19:56.992 --> 00:20:01.957
The program hasn't found a FedRAMP rule
or a FISMA provision that mandates

325
00:20:01.957 --> 00:20:08.468
private connectivity to Microsoft 365, and
TIC 3.0 mandates no specific technology.

326
00:20:08.468 --> 00:20:12.104
Write it into a task order before
the authorization is in hand,

327
00:20:12.104 --> 00:20:14.243
and you may find the gate afterwards.

328
00:20:14.243 --> 00:20:18.115
But the process is a good template
for every request you make:

329
00:20:18.115 --> 00:20:20.593
a named requirement, quoted, with a link.

330
00:20:20.593 --> 00:20:25.793
Second, the scope arguments the 2026
rules allow each come with a limit.

331
00:20:25.793 --> 00:20:30.690
Security category can vary by resource,
but that binds the provider's description;

332
00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:35.155
your own system is still categorized
under the Risk Management Framework.

333
00:20:35.155 --> 00:20:39.093
Agents are outside FedRAMP, but
not outside your authorization.

334
00:20:39.093 --> 00:20:40.962
Routine changes go unnotified,

335
00:20:40.962 --> 00:20:44.268
but you still decide whether
a change affects your risk.

336
00:20:44.268 --> 00:20:47.593
And you can't simply demand
more than FedRAMP requires.

337
00:20:47.593 --> 00:20:51.481
Acquisition is where an exception
path gets closed by accident.

338
00:20:51.481 --> 00:20:56.360
Microsoft's Network Provider Program is
no longer open to new network providers,

339
00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:59.055
so a solicitation that
names membership as a

340
00:20:59.055 --> 00:21:02.405
qualification restricts
competition to a closed pool.

341
00:21:02.405 --> 00:21:04.680
Specify the capability instead.

342
00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.491
Write the requirement the
register row describes,

343
00:21:07.491 --> 00:21:09.967
not the product that
happens to meet it today.

344
00:21:09.967 --> 00:21:13.766
The system owner decides whether
to make a provider-side request,

345
00:21:13.766 --> 00:21:16.368
and acquisition and
engineering do the work.

346
00:21:16.368 --> 00:21:20.080
That decision belongs before
the solicitation is written.

347
00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:24.442
The third response is a different
certified service for the workload.

348
00:21:24.442 --> 00:21:26.951
Chapter 10 works through collaboration,

349
00:21:26.951 --> 00:21:31.156
and its first rule is to read the
certification, not the announcement.

350
00:21:31.156 --> 00:21:35.125
On the Marketplace, Google
Workspace is certified at Class D.

351
00:21:35.125 --> 00:21:38.936
Gemini for Government is a
separate listing at Class B, Low,

352
00:21:38.936 --> 00:21:44.018
even though Google says Gemini in
Workspace apps is FedRAMP High authorized.

353
00:21:44.018 --> 00:21:48.943
Which AI features sit inside which
certification is a question for the package.

354
00:21:48.943 --> 00:21:54.694
And the Workspace listing marks some services
as not meeting a control enhancement,

355
00:21:54.694 --> 00:21:57.980
SA-9 five, on where processing
and storage happen.

356
00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:00.592
Details like those decide
what you can turn on.

357
00:22:00.592 --> 00:22:02.942
The public record shows both outcomes.

358
00:22:02.942 --> 00:22:08.835
In September 2025 a cabinet-level department
awarded a five-year agreement worth up

359
00:22:08.835 --> 00:22:14.138
to 89 million dollars to move more than
50,000 employees to Google Workspace,

360
00:22:14.138 --> 00:22:18.768
under a GSA agreement with a
temporary 71 percent price reduction.

361
00:22:18.768 --> 00:22:23.692
So a five-year cost model has to price
the renewal years, not the launch price.

362
00:22:23.692 --> 00:22:28.066
A military department went the
other way, ending about 150,000

363
00:22:28.066 --> 00:22:32.781
Google email licenses after an
audit found about 16 percent in use.

364
00:22:32.781 --> 00:22:36.142
Adoption has to be measured
in the pilot, not assumed.

365
00:22:36.142 --> 00:22:39.942
The choice is only safe if
the identity plane stays put.

366
00:22:39.942 --> 00:22:45.139
Entra ID remains the source, and the suite
is a workload that consumes identity:

367
00:22:45.139 --> 00:22:49.617
provisioned, federated, with
Conditional Access applied at sign-in.

368
00:22:49.617 --> 00:22:51.218
Then close two gaps.

369
00:22:51.218 --> 00:22:54.367
Google super administrators
sign in outside it,

370
00:22:54.367 --> 00:22:58.068
and Google's own directory is
derived, so report its drift.

371
00:22:58.068 --> 00:23:02.167
The sponsor and CIO decide
which services the agency uses.

372
00:23:02.167 --> 00:23:07.082
Make the suite decision its own approval,
separate from identity modernization,

373
00:23:07.082 --> 00:23:10.300
and read the listing again
on the day you decide.

374
00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:13.925
Appendix A turns the book into requests.

375
00:23:13.925 --> 00:23:16.662
"Give us commercial
parity" can't be answered.

376
00:23:16.662 --> 00:23:20.206
A specific question tied
to a published rule or a

377
00:23:20.206 --> 00:23:23.662
published exclusion can,
with a yes, a no or a date.

378
00:23:23.662 --> 00:23:27.876
Three habits make a request
hard to deflect: name one item,

379
00:23:27.876 --> 00:23:32.000
cite the basis with a link, and
ask for data, not documents.

380
00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:33.462
Eight requests follow.

381
00:23:33.462 --> 00:23:36.549
Publish the per-feature
exclusion list as data.

382
00:23:36.549 --> 00:23:39.762
Name the exception path
for each excluded feature.

383
00:23:39.762 --> 00:23:45.812
Restore the network observability layer
in GCC, or publish the specific obstacle.

384
00:23:45.812 --> 00:23:48.694
Explain the two same-named GCC packages,

385
00:23:48.694 --> 00:23:52.450
and which exclusions Class
D would and wouldn't lift.

386
00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:56.500
List the agents and clients that
sit outside the certified offering.

387
00:23:56.500 --> 00:24:02.651
Provide a FedRAMP-compatible trust center
with programmatic access: on October 21,

388
00:24:02.651 --> 00:24:08.713
2026, none of the three Microsoft 365
government packages had a trust-center entry.

389
00:24:08.713 --> 00:24:11.637
Deliver change notices as a JSON feed.

390
00:24:11.637 --> 00:24:15.164
And document the cryptographic
modules for each service,

391
00:24:15.164 --> 00:24:17.000
with their validation status.

392
00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:22.362
Five of the eight rest on obligations the
2026 rules already place on providers,

393
00:24:22.362 --> 00:24:25.125
so none of them needs a boundary argument.

394
00:24:25.125 --> 00:24:26.900
And asking isn't requiring.

395
00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:30.412
Requiring more than FedRAMP
does takes a determination

396
00:24:30.412 --> 00:24:33.700
by the agency head or a
delegate, and notice to FedRAMP.

397
00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:37.050
The system owner makes these
requests, with acquisition.

398
00:24:37.050 --> 00:24:40.825
Put them to your provider once
your feature inventory exists,

399
00:24:40.825 --> 00:24:45.562
and record each request, its date and
the answer in the authorization package.

400
00:24:45.562 --> 00:24:48.400
A documented no is evidence too.

401
00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:50.825
Finally, the package.

402
00:24:50.825 --> 00:24:53.837
An agency never authorizes
a cloud service;

403
00:24:53.837 --> 00:24:56.850
it authorizes its own
system, which uses one.

404
00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:59.975
The provider's certification
is evidence you read.

405
00:24:59.975 --> 00:25:02.107
Your package is evidence you write,

406
00:25:02.107 --> 00:25:06.225
and its plan should contain only
what the agency is responsible for.

407
00:25:06.225 --> 00:25:08.484
FedRAMP's Rev5 guidance is blunt:

408
00:25:08.484 --> 00:25:12.583
don't copy and paste provider
controls into the agency plan,

409
00:25:12.583 --> 00:25:18.188
and don't blindly copy provider findings
into your plan of action and milestones.

410
00:25:18.188 --> 00:25:20.863
The program writes the boundary down once,

411
00:25:20.863 --> 00:25:25.375
as a boundary record the system owner
keeps, and every artifact cites it.

412
00:25:25.375 --> 00:25:30.000
The system description names the boundary
the authorizing official determined.

413
00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.325
The external services
section names each certified

414
00:25:33.325 --> 00:25:36.575
service with its Marketplace
package, class and SKU.

415
00:25:36.575 --> 00:25:40.018
Each crossing, such as a
federated trust or a connector,

416
00:25:40.018 --> 00:25:42.925
is an information exchange
with an agreement.

417
00:25:42.925 --> 00:25:46.313
Gaps you'll close go on the
plan of action and milestones.

418
00:25:46.313 --> 00:25:49.912
Each equivalent is described
as a component in OSCAL.

419
00:25:49.912 --> 00:25:53.003
When the probe finds a
change, one record changes,

420
00:25:53.003 --> 00:25:55.862
and every narrative that
cites it is current.

421
00:25:55.862 --> 00:25:57.513
Know whose package you're reading.

422
00:25:57.513 --> 00:26:02.615
The GCC listing says the service leverages
Azure Government as its infrastructure,

423
00:26:02.615 --> 00:26:07.062
so inheritance runs through two provider
packages before it reaches you.

424
00:26:07.062 --> 00:26:10.816
And on October 21, 2026, that
listing's trust-center and

425
00:26:10.816 --> 00:26:16.276
secure-configuration fields were empty,
so record where you got the guidance.

426
00:26:16.276 --> 00:26:20.799
A compensating narrative takes four
sentences: what the control needs;

427
00:26:20.799 --> 00:26:24.786
what the provider documents as
unavailable, with the citation;

428
00:26:24.786 --> 00:26:29.463
what runs instead, marked derived; and
where the remaining gap is recorded.

429
00:26:29.463 --> 00:26:32.565
The system owner builds this
before the package reaches

430
00:26:32.565 --> 00:26:36.150
the authorizing official, and
keeps it current from then on.

431
00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.800
So, five decisions, each with its owner.

432
00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:44.570
The authorizing official determines
the boundary before workloads arrive,

433
00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:47.775
and signs every exception
with an end or review date.

434
00:26:47.775 --> 00:26:52.296
The system owner keeps the boundary
record, gates every new subscription,

435
00:26:52.296 --> 00:26:56.075
and checks every feature dependency
before a design is built.

436
00:26:56.075 --> 00:27:01.463
The CISO names the reviewer of provider
change notices and ongoing certification

437
00:27:01.463 --> 00:27:06.313
reports, and decides what counts as
evidence, with the ISSO doing the reading.

438
00:27:06.313 --> 00:27:11.299
The sponsor and CIO fund the measurement
and the compensations the register calls

439
00:27:11.299 --> 00:27:15.925
for, and plan against the class you actually
have, not the one you're waiting for.

440
00:27:15.925 --> 00:27:19.874
And the contracting officer buys
the SKU the boundary assumes,

441
00:27:19.874 --> 00:27:22.912
and specifies capabilities,
not program badges.

442
00:27:22.912 --> 00:27:24.825
The next step needs no award.

443
00:27:24.825 --> 00:27:27.092
Before the next architecture review,

444
00:27:27.092 --> 00:27:31.847
list every cloud feature your designs
depend on, with its Marketplace package,

445
00:27:31.847 --> 00:27:35.650
its availability in your environment
and the page that says so.

446
00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:38.520
Then put Appendix A's
requests to your provider,

447
00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:40.875
and record the answers in your package.

448
00:27:40.875 --> 00:27:45.429
The rules become mandatory
on January 1, 2027,

449
00:27:45.429 --> 00:27:49.613
and new Rev5 requests
stop on June 11, 2027.

450
00:27:49.613 --> 00:27:52.075
Neither date moves your own boundary.

451
00:27:52.075 --> 00:27:55.662
It's still yours to draw,
to evidence and to sign.

452
00:27:55.662 --> 00:27:59.662
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

453
00:27:59.662 --> 00:28:03.711
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 10 has the detail,

454
00:28:03.711 --> 00:28:05.574
with every claim footnoted.

455
00:28:05.574 --> 00:28:06.390
Thank you.
